# DESCRIPTION
# Problem from Functions Modeling Change, Connally et al., 3rd ed.
# WeBWorK problem written by Adam Spiegler, <aspiegler@luc.edu>
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Percents)
## DBsection(Calculations)
## Institution(Loyola University Chicago and Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Adam Spiegler and Paul Pearson)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Functions Modeling Change')
## AuthorText1('Connally')
## EditionText1('3')
## Section1('3.1')
## Problem1('6')
## KEYWORDS('exponential functions','growth rate','growth factor')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$q       = random(200, 900, 100);
$r       = random(75,  100, 5);
$decimal = $r / 100;
$b       = 1 + $decimal;
$amt     = $q * $b;

BEGIN_PGML
You start with [$q] items.  How many do you have after a [$r]% increase?
  
You will have [_]{$amt} items.

[@  helpLink('numbers')  @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For a [$r]% increase, the growth factor is _b_ = [$b], and you will have [`[$q] ([$b]) = [$amt] `] items.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
